"""Complete-exchange decisions for the art collectors and candid shopkeeper."""
REVISIONS={
    6:('Hm?',"Restore the source question to the Sailor's puzzled opening. The predecessor's exclamation suggests an assertion rather than noticing an unexpected commotion. This remains a small observing reaction,not a named player character's speech."),
    18:("Yes. He'll be an artist people remember for generations.","Preserve the Guildmaster's confident prediction of lasting fame and explicit acknowledgment. 'Famous one day' loses the source's posterity/lasting-name point. This remains the collector's judgment,not narrator-confirmed greatness or an artist biography."),
    25:("You should get one of his works now, too.","Restore the recommendation to the other collector and the source's 'too.' Remove 'while you can,'which suggests scarcity or a closing opportunity not stated by the source. The advice follows the prediction of fame without promising future prices or profit."),
    32:("That one's not bad, but it's no match for his work.","Keep the concession to the other painting and the stronger dismissive comparison expressed by かなわない. The Guildmaster politely qualifies his rival's choice while remaining convinced of his favored artist's superiority. No objective narrator judgment is added."),
    35:("What are you two talking about? That one over there is obviously better!","Restore the Priest's interruption addressed to both collectors and the indicated alternative. 'Obviously better' preserves his categorical confidence without changing the comparison into an absolute best-of-all claim. No painting title,artist name or left/right direction is invented."),
    39:("No matter what you say, I'm buying his painting!","Restore the Guildmaster's defiance and decision to buy. The predecessor's ownership claim omits the resistance to objections and can suggest an already completed acquisition. This is a spoken purchase decision,not proof of a price or inventory transaction."),
    42:("I'll take one by the artist who painted this!","Preserve the Shipwright's choice of a work by the artist responsible for the piece being indicated. The predecessor collapses that relation into a generic ownership claim. The English is a natural shop declaration without naming the artist or choosing a particular unshown inventory item."),
    45:("I'll take this one!","Render the Priest's selection as a natural buying declaration. The source announces an intended choice rather than stating completed ownership. Keep the brisk parallel with the preceding two choices without adding a reason,title or price."),
    57:("Gone at last. I wish they wouldn't argue in my shop every single time.","Keep the shopkeeper's relief and repeated-visits complaint while restoring the explicit wish that the arguing stop. The private irritation contrasts with the earlier polite thanks and sets up the Sailor's question. 'Every single time' is source-supported emphasis,not an invented visit count."),
    69:('Rich folks at play?',"Restore the questioning echo of the entire rich-person pastime idea. The predecessor asks only about 'the rich,'losing what surprises the Sailor. Repeating the retained preceding idiom makes this exchange natural and preserves the source's conversational rhythm."),
    73:("Yeah. Collecting paintings is popular these days.","Preserve the shopkeeper's casual explanation of a recent collecting fashion. Remove the unsupported universal 'everyone.' Use paintings for the specific works under discussion;the closing trade notice separately uses the approved collective label Artwork. No city-specific active boom or demand mechanic is asserted here."),
    76:("Huh. That's a pretty highbrow hobby.","Restore the Sailor's interested reaction and the hobby's cultural refinement in natural American English. 'A lofty hobby' sounds translated. 'Highbrow' conveys the source's cultivated/intellectual sense without adding an expensive purchase,expertise claim or a fixed sarcastic intent."),
    80:("Well, not many of them really have an eye for it.","Preserve the shopkeeper's dry qualification:discerning collectors are few. The predecessor's 'know nothing about art' overstates ignorance and loses the specific idea of an eye for quality. The art referent is clear from the full exchange;the line remains his opinion,not an objective judgment of every buyer."),
    83:("Hahaha! You don't mince words, do you?","Keep the Sailor's hearty laughter and comment on the shopkeeper's candor. 'You said it!' changes that into simple agreement with the criticism. The idiom reproduces the amused response to frank speech and leads naturally into the request for discretion."),
    87:("Oops. Keep that between us, will you?","Keep the already natural request but restore the source's caught-myself reaction. That small turn matters after the Sailor laughs at the shopkeeper's bluntness. The shopkeeper seeks discretion;no threat,relationship or secret beyond the spoken criticism is invented."),
    95:('Artwork may become popular in Basra.',"Use the exact approved collective trade label Artwork and city Basra. Preserve the tentative future fashion forecast without the predecessor's unproved boom wording. The notice does not establish the preceding shop location,an already earned boom,shortage,price change or guaranteed profit.")
}
RETAIN_NOTES={
    10:"Retain 'Truly marvelous!' as fluent,enthusiastic praise with a slightly polished register that fits the Guildmaster's confident art judgment. It follows the Sailor's puzzled reaction and introduces the argument without inventing an artist identity or objective narrator verdict.",
    14:"Retain 'Really?' as a natural skeptical reply to the Guildmaster's praise. The Shipwright's flat acknowledgment and later competing preference confirm that this is questioning enthusiasm,not agreement. The short English question preserves that conversational function.",
    21:"Retain 'Oh.' as the Shipwright's restrained acknowledgment after the prediction of lasting fame. The subsequent advice and rival preference make its lack of enthusiasm clear;adding admiration or a new question would change the exchange.",
    28:"Retain 'This one's better, if you ask me!' as idiomatic,blunt disagreement. The English idiom expresses 俺は...と思うがね without adding a literal request to be asked. The demonstrative stays unassigned to a title,artist name or invented physical direction.",
    49:"Retain 'Thanks for your business!' as conventional,natural American shop-service language. The Japanese service formula does not need a literal 'every time';repeat visits are explicitly preserved in the later complaint. Its public politeness supplies the intended contrast with the shopkeeper's private irritation.",
    53:"Retain 'Slam!' as a compact closing/impact sound,consistent with バタンッ and the next remark that the customers have left. It names no unmentioned object and remains an impersonal FE sound with no speaking actor. Do not convert it into narration or a shopkeeper utterance.",
    61:"Retain 'What was all that?' as natural American speech asking about the just-observed commotion. The previous argument and complaint make the referent clear. An explicit English 'shopkeeper' vocative is unnecessary here and omits no relationship or rank information.",
    65:"Retain 'Rich folks at play.' as a natural,dry localization of 金持ちの道楽 in the shopkeeper's private explanation. It conveys a pastime indulged in by wealthy people without inventing a sale price or judging the works objectively. The next turn now echoes the whole idea as in the source.",
    91:"Retain 'Of course.' as the Sailor's easy assurance in response to the request for secrecy. It conveys 分かってるよ naturally in context without adding a promise of some separate action or a new personal relationship."
}
